flying home, drawn every 10 ms of the plan (each 104 ms wingbeat at ten moments), never cut off by the screen's edge or its layer's (since round six A's woodpecker, on every screen, tablets and desktops too, with no list of known cut-offs; before, at eight moments of each wingbeat, and only where the perch decided it; a single moment missed a wing past the edge); on its side under 600 px wide (480×320, 540×320, 600×360), perched under the board or beside it, never over a cell; held upright at every 5 px of height in between (320 px wide from 460 to 540 px tall; 375, 390 and 414 px wide from 545 to 640), and at 414×622, 412×604 and 375×553, the woodpecker perched never over a cell, 24 px tall or more and all on the screen, and the board bigger than before; in a browser without `svh` and `dvh` (the page's stylesheet with them made unknown), the board, its frame and the ledge as they are with them; the full screen button, in and out on a real tap, and none where it isn't allowed; the wake lock, through a stand-in that records every request and release, through the countdown, play, a nap, the Hall of Fame, a hidden tab, a lock the browser let go, your death's card, keeping watching, the round's end, a watched AI round, a refusal, and a browser with none; a real touch swipe on the board, upright and on its side; and no error events on the page, playing, watching or resized (a resize observer's loop isn't an exception, so it was never caught: the game sets what's above the board as the window changes, not from inside one's call). Each reading waits until the page has been drawn and its own resize observers have run (a fixed wait read it too early now and then on a busy machine).
+## Your snake talks (a prototype after round six A)
+
+The owner, after playing: the lessons "flash before you, too quick to really register", "in your face in the middle of the screen" on a phone, while you’re playing a fast game; "can dialogue bubbles be used to teach you? We already got them nailed… better game design to show players what they can do and shouldn’t do, rather than flash them in the eyes with signs." And the woodpecker is "very small on the phone". Measured first (18 rounds as a new player, three screens): a lesson was up 2.5 to 3.2 s for 9 to 15 words, banners were up 39 to 50% of play, lessons came in chains of up to seven in 18 s at 2:00, a third of a phone’s lessons sat mid-board, and the perched bird was 38 to 52 px tall on a phone against 136 on a desktop.
+
+**What happens to you is said by your own snake.** The rules that were lesson banners are lines your own snake says, in the first person, in a speech bubble at its head, as the rivals say theirs (they mirror the rivals’ own lines for the same moments: a rival chokes "Ptooey!", yours "Pthoo! That’s my own tail."). The words are where your eyes already are, and nothing crosses the board.
+- *Its voice:* plain and plucky, a newcomer to the log learning it alongside you. One idea a line, 32 characters at most, so a phone’s bubble always shows it whole (the rivals’ longer lines go to the log on small boards). It names a key only when it asks you to press it ("Tap, and I’ll hop that!"), and only until you have.
+- *When:* the moment it happens, once a round, and only the first two rounds you meet each rule on this device (`snake-voice` in local storage: a rule told is told; the banners came every round, and hunger up to five times one). Then the board’s own pictures and the Field guide carry it. One line at a time: a line that can’t show while another is being read isn’t spent, so the next time teaches it; what just hurt you, or what you just got, takes over; and the cough’s line waits for the choke’s.
+- *How long:* its reading time, read in glances while you steer: 1.2 s and 0.45 s a word, 2.4 to 4.2 s (a banner was up 2.5 s for 15 words). It pops in from its tail (no slam), and holds still with reduced motion.
+- *Where:* the rivals’ bubble, edged in your colour with an ink hairline, a touch bolder and bigger (bigger again on a big board). It keeps clear of your head, the three cells ahead and the two of a turn you’ve pressed, the banner, and what it’s about (the bead you spat, the pit you bridge, the woodpecker’s mark). It stays put while that’s all still clear; when it must move it goes across the way you’ve been going (above or below a snake going sideways, beside one going up or down, and on a staircase the corner off to its side, clear of the way ahead and of the body behind), fading over rather than gliding across your head. It never fades for your head; the rivals’ make way for it. It’s in the log too, under your name, when the chatter’s on, and screen readers hear it; with the chatter off it still shows (it’s how the game teaches you).
+
+**What you could do, shown.** Two pictures, drawn while your snake asks for the press, until you’ve done it yourself twice: the jump’s way over what’s ahead (a dotted arc in the jump’s sky from your head over the hurdle to a ring on the cell it would land on: faint and still while it’s coming, from five cells; bright, its dots marching and its ring breathing once a press now would go, within the jump’s wait), and the flash’s two ember chevrons on the two cells it would cover, when your flash first comes ready. Both are inked so they read on bark, fire and snow.
+
+**Banners are the round’s news only** (the woodpecker, first blood, the last two, the weather, a spawn, land the woodpecker took, the last apples): one line on any phone where it can be ("The woodpecker is here."), held for its reading time (1 s and 0.4 s a word, up to 4.5 s), nothing cutting it short but a card, never two back to back (half a second’s breather), and never in the board’s middle: on a phone held upright it’s off the board, just above it, over the tags’ lower row; elsewhere at the board’s top or foot. It settles in with a short drop and fade: no slam from 1.9 times its size, no red throb, no shine running across the gold.
+
+**The woodpecker, full size on a phone.** On a phone held upright it clings to the chatter’s wood under the ledge, its crown still just under the board, down to the foot of the chatter’s head row, beside its title and its switch (never over either), in front of the moss hanging off the log: 78 px tall at 390×844 (was 52), 72 at 360×640 (was 38), 4.9 cells, a fifth of the board, as on a desktop or a phone on its side, its eye 5.2 to 5.7 px. So it no longer grows as it takes off to drum. Watching, the speed buttons between keep it on the ledge, as before.
+
 ## Skipped or adapted guidelines
 
 Each line gives the guideline, what was done, and why.
